                Getting Help

                If in doubt about a CLI command, pseudomacro, or macro,
                you can ask the CLI to display an explanation of the item.

                To display very brief help for a command, type HELP followed
                by the item's name.  For example

                ) HELP DELETE <NL>

                The resulting display reports whether the DELETE command
                accepts or requires arguments, and lists the switches you
                can use with the command.

                Often, though, you'll need more verbose help.  Then
                you can either add the /V switch to the HELP command
        |       (HELP/V TIME) or use the HELPV macro (HELPV TIME).  The
        |       HELPV macro works the same way as the HELP/V command.
        |
        |       For help with macros or utility programs, type HELP,
        |       HELP/V, or HELPV followed by the item's name prefixed
        |       with an asterisk (HELP/V *BROWSE).  For help with
        |       pseudomacros, prefix the item's name with an exclamation
        |       point (HELPV !END).
        |
        |       If you are using CLI32, you can use the HELPB and XHELPB
        |       macros that let you display help information through the
        |       BROWSE utility.
        |
	|       For CLI commands, macros, or pseudomacros, the format is
        |
        |       HELPB  {command      }
        |              {*topic       }
        |              {!pseudomacro }
        |
        |       For EXEC commands, the format is
        |
        |       XHELPB  [command]
        |
        |
        |       When you use HELPB or XHELPB, only the first screen
        |       displays initially.  You can use Next Screen (F4) and
        |       Previous Screen (F3) to view the information a screen at a
        |       time.  Use the up- or downarrow keys to display an additional
        |       line (or lines if you hold down the key) in either direction.
        |       Press Cancel/Exit (F11) or BREAK/ESC to exit.  For more
        |       information, refer to the description of the BROWSE utility
        |       in this chapter, or type HELPB *BROWSE.




