	CLEARDEVICE
     |     Initiates Dump/Reboot of an IAC controller or engine, or simulates
     |     a CTRL-Q (X-ON) from a device, or sends a break sequence to a device.
        ________________________________________________________________________

	Format

	CLEARDEVICE/switch device

     |  Depending on the switch you use, the command initiates Dump/Reboot of an
     |  IAC controller or engine, or simulates a CTRL-Q (X-ON) from the
        specified device, or sends a break character to the device.  The device
        argument must be the name of a console line; for example @CON12.

        *  No Templates.

	*  No argument switches.

     |  *  Requirement:  /RCNTL and /RENG require SYSTEMMANAGER privilege
     |                   or PID 2, and are for AOS/VS II only;
     |                   /RXON and /SBREAK require that you either
     |                   be logged onto the terminal device, or you have
     |                   SYSTEMMANAGER privilege, or you are PID 2.


        Why Use It?

     |  The /RCNTL or /RENG switch can be used when you want to initiate a
     |  Dump/Reboot of an IAC controller or engine.  Refer to the manual
     |  "Managing AOS/VS and AOS/VS II", part number 093-000541, for more
     |  information; see section "Operator Initiated IAC Reboot" in the
     |  chapter titled "Improving System Availability".
     |
     |  The /RXON switch can be used to simulate receipt of a CTRL-Q character
     |  from a device that is using CTRL-S (X-OFF) and CTRL-Q (X-ON) characters
     |  for flow control.  For example, a printer attached to a console line is
     |  normally configured to use X-OFF/X-ON flow control.  If this printer
     |  goes off-line while in the X-OFF state, it may not work properly when
     |  the printer comes back on-line.  If the system does not resume sending
     |  data to the printer because it is waiting for an X-ON character, the
     |  CLEARDEVICE/RXON command may be used to clear this state.
     |
     |  The /SBREAK switch can be used to send a break sequence to a device.
        Some types of communications hardware require you to send a break
        sequence before you can gain access to the device.

